From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Eric Reinsmidt <735251+ericreinsmidt@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] The XR829's Wi-Fi driver takes a locally administered address
 as its macaddr= parameter: it turned down any address with that bit set,
 which is the right kind for one the system makes up, so plastron can give
 each Brick one fixed address at load. The chip is given its address only
 then: one set on the interface later reaches the frames but not the chip,
 which went on listening for its old address and never heard the router

---
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/xr829/wlan/main.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/xr829/wlan/main.c
@@ -457,11 +457,14 @@
 
 /*bit0: 1=multicast, 0=unicast*/
 /*bit1: 1=locally, 0=universally(vendor use)*/
+/* Unicast is all a station's address needs: a locally administered one is
+ * the right kind for an address the system makes up, as plastron does for
+ * the Brick from its chip's serial, and passes as macaddr= */
 #define MACADDR_VAILID(a) ( \
 (a[0] != 0 || a[1] != 0 ||  \
  a[2] != 0 || a[3] != 0 ||  \
  a[4] != 0 || a[5] != 0) && \
- !(a[0] & 0x3))
+ !(a[0] & 0x1))
 
 static void xradio_get_mac_addrs(u8 *macaddr)
 {
